From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 18:47:15 -0500
Subject: fix: harden the Arc importer

The sidebar parser dropped in silence whatever it could not read: a note, an
Easel, a kind it did not know and everything in it, a page that could not be
opened, a name with no item behind it, an entry with no id, and all that lay
sixteen lists down. Each is now counted, with what it holds, and the summary, the
result line and the page after an import say how many. A list among the favorites
is counted too, and so are the favorites past the twelve, which were dropped at
the first one over.

Each item now comes across once in the whole file, not once a container, so a
file cannot make a tree of Spaces times items. Cycles of lists already ended, and
still do, with a test now. What lies past the depth is counted without recursion:
a chain of lists can be as long as the file.

Arc's history and passwords no longer depend on the sidebar. With no sidebar file,
or one that does not parse, they still come, the summary and the result line say
the sidebar could not be read, and the settings row and the command bar offer the
import. A section's word is read as public exporters meet it too, as a dict's only
key, the list as newContainerIDs, and a second container of a section adds to the
first instead of replacing it.

With Spaces unticked the pins and tabs now come, into the Space on screen. They
were dropped with the Spaces, whatever their own boxes said. The settings row says
when Arc is running, as the welcome block does. PBKDF2 is upstream's, through a
pass key of its own, in place of a loop written here; the RFC's vectors hold.

Parsing was already off the UI thread at all three ways in, Import sidebar
already merged by durable ids, and Export Space already kept its name and
isolation and wrote a file of its own: the row was out of date there. The Mac's
keychain query stays as it was: it cannot be built here.

Why: what cannot be read should be said, and a broken sidebar should not cost a
person their history (BACKLOG S-160, import I1, I4, I6, I9, I11, I21).

Removable when: Arc's files are gone from people's computers.
